//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.json — JSON as a QUERY, over the s40 kernel builtins: is this
//! text valid, what is at this path, what kind is it, how many children
//! does it have, and give it to me as the wolf type I expect.
//! Stability tier: **x (nursery, D31)**.
//!
//! ## The nursery banner (D31) — read this before you depend on it
//!
//! **`std.x.*` is not `std.*`.** A module under `x` is a RESIDENT rather
//! than a facade member — its path, its names, its rows and its behaviour
//! may change or disappear in a single sprint, and no other std module may
//! depend on one. `std/x/README.md` is the register: every resident is
//! listed there with the measured reason it is not in the facade and the
//! named trigger that graduates it.
//!
//! **The graduation clock.** At every campaign closeout each resident is
//! reviewed and one of three things happens, recorded in that closeout:
//! it GRADUATES (it moves under `std.`, and because the path *is* the
//! API, the move is the release note), it is DELETED (the reviewed
//! contract in its header is what survives), or it is EXTENDED by one
//! campaign with a written reason. Nothing sits in `x` unexamined.
//!
//! **This module's clock** starts at the stdc02 closeout, which is this
//! campaign's. Its named triggers are below.
//!
//! ## Why the first tenant is json
//!
//! D31 said the nursery's first tenant would be a json module, and s40
//! built the kernel for it — but the reason it is a resident rather than
//! a member is measured, not decreed, and it is two reasons:
//!
//! 1. **Unequal lanes — and the count MOVED at sc15.** `json_valid`/
//!    `json_get`/`json_type`/`json_len` were the checked tier's alone
//!    from s40 until this sprint's pin. s107 crossed the family natively
//!    (`wolf_rt::json`, a hand mirror of `wolf_mem::json` — the locked
//!    crate graph forbids reuse — with the two copies pinned upstream by
//!    the driver's json_parity test), so BOTH compiler rungs execute
//!    every function here now. lupin still declines the surface by
//!    design ("rather than risk a second, guessed RFC 8259 reading")
//!    while wolf-interp's own reading lands (is18, concurrent with this
//!    sprint); its column flips on a pin bump, never on an edit here.
//!    The graduation trigger was the native mirror plus the
//!    interpreter's own — the same two-consumer shape every builtin tier
//!    goes through. The first clause FIRED at this pin; the second is in
//!    flight, and the register records both.
//! 2. **This is a query surface, not the API json will end up with.** A
//!    dotted path is a small language and it cannot express what the
//!    document can hold: a key containing `.` is unreachable at this tier
//!    (documented upstream and here), there is no way to enumerate an
//!    object's keys, and every read re-parses the whole text. The API a
//!    program eventually wants is `std.json`'s DOM — one parse, a value
//!    you navigate — and that module has been complete in both
//!    directions since sc14 (see the sibling note below). What THIS
//!    module grew at sc15 is the compiled lanes' counterpart: the `Node`
//!    half below, a navigable handle over the same four kernels, because
//!    `std.json`'s `Value` is an enum only the interpreter executes
//!    (F-0029) and the kernels now execute everywhere else. Whether a
//!    query face lives on beside the DOM or is deleted in favour of it
//!    is still a closeout's decision, and this is where it is written
//!    down.
//!
//! ## `std.x.json` and `std.json` are two modules, and what separates
//! ## them MOVED at this pin
//!
//! For five sprints module identity was the LAST path segment (F-0034),
//! so `use std.json` beside `use std.x.json` was `E0306` on both
//! compiler rungs (F-0058's import half). **s108 probe-closed that**
//! (wolf-lang#29): identity is the FULL path now, and the pair
//! RESOLVES on all three lanes — the leaf-name clash is only a binding
//! clash, and `use std.x.json as xj` names the second binding, exactly
//! as the E0306 suggestion says. Measured at these pins, both
//! directions: what stops a co-importing program today is the LANES,
//! not the names — it dies at the first `std.json` body on the compiler
//! rungs ("methods on generic std data") and at the first `json_*`
//! under lupin. So every test and doc example here still imports
//! exactly one module, and the two halves still compose at the TEXT
//! level: `text_of` of a container is a document `std.json.parse`
//! reads, and `stringify` writes a document `root` reads.
//!
//! They divide the work the way their lanes do. `std.json` owns the DOM —
//! a `Value` enum you build, NAVIGATE and serialize. Navigation arrived
//! there at sc13: F-0037 (an enum returned through an error row takes the
//! miss path on every call) closed at lupin 0.1.10 and `json.get`/`at`
//! ship, so the sentence this paragraph carried for three sprints —
//! "the DOM cannot be walked" — is retired; `parse` arrived at sc14 and
//! the DOM is complete in both directions. This module owns the read
//! direction — text in, wolf values out — and cannot build anything,
//! because the kernel is queries only. The lanes divide the same way the
//! work does: `std.json`'s `Value` executes on the interpreter alone
//! (F-0029), while everything here executes exactly where the kernels do
//! — both compiler rungs at this sprint's pin, lupin pending is18.
//!
//! **Which means the graduation question this module's clock asks gets
//! sharper rather than easier.** The register says `x.json` either
//! graduates as the streaming QUERY face beside `std.json`'s DOM or is
//! deleted in favour of it; the DOM answered `parse` at sc14, and at
//! sc15 this module answered back with the `Node` half, so the question
//! is symmetric now: two complete read surfaces, one per lane family.
//! Nothing about the banner, the clock or the trigger changes here —
//! this is a note for whoever holds the closeout, written where they
//! will be standing.
//!
//! ## The tier this delegates to
//!
//! **Capability-free.** The `json_*` builtins are the one pure builtin
//! family: no I13 capability, no sandbox category, nothing reached. A
//! package that uses only this module and the language declares no
//! capability at all. The comptime engine still refuses them — there is
//! no json evaluator in the D33 allowlist at v0, which is a design
//! decision and not a capability question.
//!
//! **And that refusal is the one thing in this repository that cannot be
//! held as a test.** `tests/{net,time,env}/comptime_refuses.lu` each pin
//! their tier's D33 rejection as `fail(E0701)` with the sandbox table's
//! own sentence ("reaches the clock, which comptime code can never
//! touch"). A `comptime fn` calling `json_valid` answers a bare
//! `unsupported` at resolve — no code, no reason string, nothing in the
//! record's `diagnostics` — so there is no directive to write it against
//! and a package author learns nothing about why. Filed as F-0060; there
//! is deliberately no `comptime_refuses.lu` here rather than a file
//! asserting something weaker than the truth.
//!
//! **Paths are dotted segments.** `""` is the root; a segment of digits
//! indexes an array; anything else keys an object. `"users.0.name"` is
//! the second element's `name`. The node's KIND is what decides — on an
//! object every segment is a key, digits included, so a member spelled
//! `"0"` is reachable (measured; `dom_navigation.lu` pins it) — which is
//! also why the DOM half's `member`/`element` can be typed at all. Build
//! a path with `child`/`child_index` rather than by hand. **A key containing `.` is unreachable** — the
//! tier says so and so does this module; it is not a bug to work around
//! here, it is the ceiling of a dotted path, and it is one of the reasons
//! the DOM is where json is going.
//!
//! **The rows are the toolchain's, verbatim** (§14's rule, and this
//! module adds no translation layer): `parse` for any RFC 8259 violation
//! in the DOCUMENT, `missing` for a path that addresses nothing, `kind`
//! for a node of the wrong kind for the operation. Two tags are this
//! module's own and they are marked as such below: `kind` is reused for
//! "the node is a number and you asked for an integer", and `overflow`
//! (§10's mark) for a number outside the wolf type you asked for.
//! Nothing here traps — a document is data, and every failure a document
//! can cause is a row.
//!
//! **Numbers keep their source spelling in the kernel** and this module
//! is where they become wolf values: `int_at` reads an integer exactly,
//! so a 64-bit id survives — `std.json`'s number posture, arrived at
//! from the other direction. The `f64` half is a contract below and the
//! reason is measured, not principled.
//!
//! **A duplicate key answers its FIRST occurrence at this tier, and the
//! duplicates COUNT.** Measured at these pins (F-0088): `get` on
//! `{"a": 1, "a": 2}` at `"a"` is `1` and `len` of that object is 2,
//! where `std.json.parse` of the same text keeps the LAST occurrence
//! and counts one member (a `Map` assignment, and the wider ecosystem's
//! last-wins reading). Both readings are RFC 8259-legal and neither
//! corpus witness pins one, so the seam is FILED for a ruling rather
//! than absorbed here, and `tests/x/json/dom_typed_reads.lu` holds the
//! measurement so a ruling flips a row instead of rotting a sentence.
//!
//! **Every read re-parses the document.** `get` on a ten-megabyte text is
//! a ten-megabyte parse, and ten reads are ten parses. That is the
//! kernel's shape, not a defect this module can hide, and it is the
//! second-loudest argument for the DOM. Read a subtree once with `get`
//! (which hands back a valid JSON text) and query THAT, if the shape of
//! your data allows it. **The `Node` half below does not change the
//! cost**: a `Node` is an ADDRESS — a document and a path — not a
//! materialization, so each accessor is one more parse. What it buys is
//! checked construction: a `Node` this module handed out addresses a
//! node that existed, in a document that parsed, with the misses raised
//! where the navigation happened rather than at every later read.
//!
//! Reviewed contracts, blocked at the tier below:
//!
//! ```text
//! float_at(doc: str, path: str) -> f64 ! {parse, missing, kind, overflow}
//!     The number at `path` as `f64`, widening an integer node, with
//!     `std.fmt.decimal.parse_float`'s correct rounding. WRITTEN, and
//!     withdrawn before it shipped, for a reason that is arithmetic
//!     rather than design: `parse_float` is `unsupported` on BOTH
//!     compiler rungs ("arithmetic outside integers" at the mem tier —
//!     F-0026's f64 ceiling, one body deep), and the checked tier is
//!     this module's ONLY executing lane. So the function would have
//!     had zero lanes and zero evidence: no test could run it, no doc
//!     example could be fenced, and §14's doc-truth floor ("at least
//!     one lane reaches exit(0)") would have been missed on every one.
//!     A std function nobody can run is a claim, not code. It lands the
//!     day either compiler rung executes `parse_float`, with this
//!     signature and a four-line body; `get` plus the caller's own
//!     conversion is the spelling until then.
//! keys(doc: str, path: str) -> List[str] ! {parse, missing, kind}
//!     An object's member names. No builtin enumerates them — `json_len`
//!     counts members and nothing names them — so an object can be
//!     counted and not walked, which is the sharpest hole in the query
//!     tier. **This is sc15's NAMED STOP**: the `Node` half below
//!     inherits the hole whole (`keys_of` is the same contract over a
//!     handle), s107 added no enumeration kernel, and the ask is written
//!     rather than worked around — `json_keys(doc, path) -> List[str]
//!     ! {parse, missing, kind}`, the `fs_read_dir` shape one tier over
//!     (F-0087, filed upstream). Blocked, not unwritten.
//! items(doc: str, path: str) -> List[str] ! {parse, missing, kind}
//!     An array's elements as sub-documents. Writable TODAY over
//!     `json_len` plus `child_index` plus `get` in a loop, and left out
//!     deliberately: it would parse the document once per element, which
//!     is quadratic in a shape programs reach for constantly, hidden
//!     behind one call. sc15's `element` reads ONE element per call, so
//!     the walk a caller writes — `len_of` plus `element` in a loop —
//!     costs the same and shows the cost at the call site. A
//!     list-returning `items` still waits for a cursor in the tier.
//! parse(doc: str) -> Value   ·   stringify(v: Value) -> str
//!     The DOM directions, both `std.json`'s. `stringify` shipped at
//!     sc05, `get`/`at` at sc13 and `parse` at sc14 — that DOM is
//!     complete. At sc15 THIS module grew its own `Node` face beside the
//!     query functions: the compiled lanes' DOM, over the kernels, with
//!     `root` as its entry point (not `parse` — see `root`'s doc for the
//!     F-0036 reason).
//! ```

use std.fmt

/// Whether `doc` is one well-formed RFC 8259 text.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps — the one function here that answers for
/// every input, which is what makes it the guard the others are written
/// behind. Strict: no comments, no trailing commas, no bare identifiers,
/// exactly one value with only insignificant whitespace around it, and
/// container nesting limited (the kernel's limit is 128, and exceeding it
/// is invalidity rather than a stack fault).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33 — no json evaluator, not a
/// capability). Delegates to `json_valid`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.is_valid("[1, 2, 3]") == true
/// json.is_valid("{{\"a\": 1}}") == true
/// json.is_valid("[1,]") == false
/// json.is_valid("{{") == false
/// json.is_valid("") == false
/// ```
pub fn is_valid(doc: str) -> bool {
    json_valid(doc)
}

/// The node at `path`, rendered as text.
///
/// A string node comes back DECODED (escapes resolved, so `"a\nb"` is two
/// lines and not four characters); a number keeps its source spelling
/// exactly, so nothing is rounded on the way out; `true`, `false` and
/// `null` spell themselves; an object or array comes back as JSON text —
/// **a valid document in its own right**, which is what makes
/// `get(get(doc, "a"), "b")` work and is the cheapest way to query a
/// subtree twice.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when `doc` is not valid JSON, `missing` when nothing is
/// at `path`. Never traps.
///
/// Bare `get` is the checked-access spelling and this one can miss (§1).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.get("{{\"a\": 1}}", "a") else "?") == "1"
/// (json.get("[10, 20]", "1") else "?") == "20"
/// (json.get("{{\"s\": \"hi\"}}", "s") else "?") == "hi"
/// (json.get("{{\"a\": {{\"b\": 2}}}}", "a.b") else "?") == "2"
/// (json.get("{{\"a\": 1}}", "b") else "?") == "?"
/// (json.get("{{", "a") else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn get(doc: str, path: str) -> str ! {parse, missing} {
    json_get(doc, path)?
}

/// The kind of the node at `path` — one of `"object"`, `"array"`,
/// `"str"`, `"num"`, `"bool"`, `"null"`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`. Never traps.
///
/// Spelled `type_name` and not `kind` for the reason `std.json` learned
/// the hard way: a function and a row tag with the same name in one
/// module make the tag resolve to the FUNCTION at the raise site,
/// silently (F-0036). The names here are the kernel's own vocabulary
/// (`"str"` and `"num"`, not RFC 8259's "string" and "number"), adopted
/// verbatim per §14 rather than prettified into a second dialect.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_type`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": [1]}}", "a") else "?") == "array"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": [1]}}", "a.0") else "?") == "num"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": \"x\"}}", "a") else "?") == "str"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": null}}", "a") else "?") == "null"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": true}}", "a") else "?") == "bool"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": {{}}}}", "a") else "?") == "object"
/// ```
pub fn type_name(doc: str, path: str) -> str ! {parse, missing} {
    json_type(doc, path)?
}

/// How many elements an array has, or members an object has, at `path`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`, and `kind` for a scalar — a number has no
/// length, and answering 1 or 0 would be a guess about which question was
/// asked. Never traps.
///
/// Not a string length: `len` of a `"str"` node is `kind`, and the length
/// of the text it holds is `std.str.len` of what `get` returns (§1: one
/// concept, one home).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_len`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.len("[1, 2, 3]", "") else -1) == 3
/// (json.len("{{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}}", "") else -1) == 2
/// (json.len("[[1, 2]]", "0") else -1) == 2
/// (json.len("[]", "") else -1) == 0
/// (json.len("[1]", "0") else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn len(doc: str, path: str) -> int ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    json_len(doc, path)?
}

/// Whether `doc` has a node at `path`.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when the document is not valid JSON — a question about a
/// path has no honest answer when the text is not a document, and `false`
/// would say "no such field" about a file that is not JSON at all.
/// Never traps.
///
/// `has` rather than `contains` for a keyed lookup (§8's vocabulary).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_valid`
/// and `json_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.has("{{\"a\": 1}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.has("{{\"a\": 1}}", "b") else false) == false
/// (json.has("{{\"a\": null}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.has("[1, 2]", "1") else false) == true
/// (json.has("[1, 2]", "9") else false) == false
/// (json.has("nope", "a") else true) == true
/// ```
pub fn has(doc: str, path: str) -> bool ! {parse} {
    if !json_valid(doc) {
        return parse
    }
    let probe = json_get(doc, path) else |_| { return false }
    probe.len >= 0
}

/// The text of the string node at `path`, decoded.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`, and `kind` when the node is not a string.
/// No coercion: `str_at` of a number is a miss and not `"42"`, because a
/// reader that guesses across JSON's types is a reader that loses the
/// document's own information. `get` is the function that renders any
/// node as text.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.str_at("{{\"a\": \"wolf\"}}", "a") else "?") == "wolf"
/// (json.str_at("{{\"a\": \"line\\nbreak\"}}", "a") else "?") == "line\nbreak"
/// (json.str_at("{{\"a\": 1}}", "a") else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn str_at(doc: str, path: str) -> str ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    if node != "str" {
        return kind
    }
    json_get(doc, path)?
}

/// The boolean at `path`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`, `kind` for every non-boolean node. No
/// coercion: `0`, `""` and `null` are all `kind`.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.bool_at("{{\"a\": true}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.bool_at("{{\"a\": false}}", "a") else true) == false
/// (json.bool_at("{{\"a\": 0}}", "a") else false) == false
/// ```
pub fn bool_at(doc: str, path: str) -> bool ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    if node != "bool" {
        return kind
    }
    let text = json_get(doc, path)?
    text == "true"
}

/// Whether the node at `path` is JSON `null`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`. A predicate rather than a coercion, because
/// "is this null" has a yes-or-no answer for every node that EXISTS —
/// while an absent node is a different question, and it is `missing`.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.is_null_at("{{\"a\": null}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.is_null_at("{{\"a\": 0}}", "a") else true) == false
/// (json.is_null_at("{{\"a\": null}}", "b") else true) == true
/// ```
pub fn is_null_at(doc: str, path: str) -> bool ! {parse, missing} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    node == "null"
}

/// The integer at `path`, exactly.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`; `kind` when the node is not a number OR is a
/// number that is not an integer (`1.5`, `1e3` and `1.0` are all `kind` —
/// the document wrote a non-integer and this function will not round
/// one); `overflow` (§10's mark) when the integer is outside `int`.
///
/// **`overflow` is a row and not a trap, and that is the whole reason
/// this function does its own range check** rather than delegating
/// straight to `std.fmt.parse_int`, which traps on an out-of-range value
/// (correctly: to `fmt` the text is a caller's argument). Here the digits
/// came out of a DOCUMENT — data, which §10 says raises where a caller's
/// mistake traps — so a 20-digit id in someone's JSON is an outcome this
/// function reports, never a process this function ends.
///
/// Exactness is the point: an id beyond 2^53 survives `int_at` and would
/// not survive `float_at`. That is the same number posture `std.json`
/// takes from the writing side.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 42}}", "a") else -1) == 42
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": -7}}", "a") else 0) == -7
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 9007199254740993}}", "a") else 0) == 9007199254740993
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 1.5}}", "a") else -1) == -1
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": \"7\"}}", "a") else -1) == -1
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 99999999999999999999}}", "a") else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn int_at(doc: str, path: str) -> int ! {parse, missing, kind, overflow} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    if node != "num" {
        return kind
    }
    let text = json_get(doc, path)?
    integer_of(text)?
}

/// `path` extended by one object key.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps, and PURE — the only member of this module
/// that reaches no builtin, so it is the only one a comptime program
/// could use. An empty `path` is the root, so `child("", "a")` is `"a"`.
///
/// A `segment` containing `.` produces a path that addresses something
/// else, and this function does not pretend otherwise: there is no
/// escape in the dotted-path language to hide behind (the module header's
/// ceiling). Keys with dots need the DOM.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.child("", "users") == "users"
/// json.child("users", "0") == "users.0"
/// json.child("a.b", "c") == "a.b.c"
/// ```
pub fn child(path: str, segment: str) -> str {
    if path == "" {
        return segment
    }
    "{path}.{segment}"
}

/// `path` extended by one array index.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps, and PURE — comptime-safe. A negative index
/// is not rejected here: it produces a path that addresses nothing, which
/// is the `missing` row at the query, and one refusal in the right place
/// beats two in the wrong one.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.child_index("", 0) == "0"
/// json.child_index("users", 2) == "users.2"
/// json.child_index("a.0", 1) == "a.0.1"
/// ```
pub fn child_index(path: str, i: int) -> str {
    child(path, "{i}")
}

/// One location in one well-formed document — the DOM half's handle
/// (sc15). A `Node` is an ADDRESS, not a materialization: a document and
/// a dotted path into it, both plain `str`, so the value crosses every
/// lane a struct crosses and costs nothing to copy but the text it
/// already shares.
///
/// **What a `Node` promises.** Every `Node` this module hands out —
/// from `root`, `node`, `member` or `element` — addressed an existing
/// node in a document that parsed, at the moment it was built; the
/// document is an immutable `str`, so that stays true. The misses are
/// therefore raised where the NAVIGATION happens, and a later read of a
/// module-built `Node` cannot miss on `parse` or `missing`.
///
/// **What a forged one does.** The fields are open — this tier buys no
/// privacy (the `std.time` posture) — so a caller can assemble a `Node`
/// out of any two strings. A forged `Node` produces ROWS and never a
/// trap: every accessor's `parse` and `missing` tags stay on the
/// signature because a forged argument can reach them, and the docs
/// below say which callers can.
///
/// **The ceiling travels with the path.** A `Node` is a dotted path, so
/// a key containing `.` is as unreachable through `member` as through
/// `child`, and for the same reason: there is no escape in the path
/// language to hide behind. Keys with dots need `std.json`'s DOM.
pub struct Node {
    doc: str,
    path: str,
}

/// The root node of a well-formed document.
///
/// The DOM half's entry point: one validation, then a handle every later
/// navigation and read composes from. Rows: `parse` when `doc` is not
/// RFC 8259 — the only miss a root can have, since the root of a valid
/// document always exists (`missing` is not on the signature because no
/// argument can reach it).
///
/// **Spelled `root` and not `parse`, and the reason is F-0036's
/// symmetric rule (sc14): a new `pub fn` is checked against its module's
/// TAGS.** This module raises the kernel's `parse` tag throughout — `has`
/// raises it by name, `root` itself does — and a tag that shares a name
/// with anything in scope resolves to that thing silently. A `pub fn
/// parse` here would capture every one of those raise sites, including
/// its own body's. `std.json` hit the same collision from the other side
/// and renamed its TAG (`syntax`); here the tag is the toolchain's
/// verbatim and cannot move, so the FUNCTION yields the name.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_valid`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = json.root("{{\"a\": 1}}")?
/// (json.type_of(r) else "?") == "object"
/// let scalar = json.root("  42  ")?
/// (json.int_of(scalar) else -1) == 42
/// ```
pub fn root(doc: str) -> Node ! {parse} {
    if !json_valid(doc) {
        return parse
    }
    Node { doc: doc, path: "" }
}

/// The node at `path` — the bridge between the two halves: a dotted path
/// built with `child`/`child_index` (or received as data) becomes a
/// handle the DOM half reads.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when `doc` is not RFC 8259, `missing` when the path
/// addresses nothing. Never traps.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_type`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let n = json.node("{{\"a\": {{\"b\": 2}}}}", "a.b")?
/// (json.int_of(n) else -1) == 2
/// let whole = json.node("[10, 20]", "")?
/// (json.len_of(whole) else -1) == 2
/// ```
pub fn node(doc: str, path: str) -> Node ! {parse, missing} {
    json_type(doc, path)?
    Node { doc: doc, path: path }
}

/// The member node at `key` of an object node.
///
/// Rows: `kind` when `n` is not an object — `member` is the TYPED
/// navigation, so asking an array or a scalar for a member is a wrong
/// kind and not a silent miss; `missing` when the object has no entry at
/// `key`; `parse` and `missing` also cover a forged `n` (see `Node`).
/// Never traps.
///
/// The predicate spelling is the query half's `has` (§1: a question with
/// a boolean answer keeps its predicate): `has(n.doc, child(n.path,
/// key))` answers without building the handle a caller does not want.
///
/// A `key` containing `.` addresses along the dots — the path ceiling,
/// stated on `Node` and held as a test, not worked around here.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_type`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = json.root("{{\"pack\": [{{\"name\": \"lupin\"}}]}}")?
/// let pack = json.member(r, "pack")?
/// let leader = json.element(pack, 0)?
/// let name = json.member(leader, "name")?
/// (json.str_of(name) else "?") == "lupin"
/// ```
pub fn member(n: Node, key: str) -> Node ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    let holder = json_type(n.doc, n.path)?
    if holder != "object" {
        return kind
    }
    let p = child(n.path, key)
    json_type(n.doc, p)?
    Node { doc: n.doc, path: p }
}

/// The element node at index `i` of an array node.
///
/// Rows: `kind` when `n` is not an array — an object with a member
/// spelled `"0"` is reached by `member`, never by `element`, so the two
/// navigations cannot be confused; `missing` when `i` is outside the
/// array (a negative `i` is the same miss and not a from-the-end read —
/// `at`'s rule in `std.json`, held here too); `parse` and `missing` also
/// cover a forged `n`. Never traps: the index came out of a walk over a
/// DOCUMENT, and §10 says data raises where a caller's mistake traps.
///
/// The traversal this module has is `len_of` plus `element` in a loop —
/// one parse per element, the kernel's cost at the call site where the
/// caller can see it. There is no iterator surface here and none is
/// coming at this tier (ruled territory).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_type`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = json.root("[10, 20, 30]")?
/// let second = json.element(r, 1)?
/// (json.int_of(second) else -1) == 20
/// ```
pub fn element(n: Node, i: int) -> Node ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    let holder = json_type(n.doc, n.path)?
    if holder != "array" {
        return kind
    }
    let p = child_index(n.path, i)
    json_type(n.doc, p)?
    Node { doc: n.doc, path: p }
}

/// The kind of the node — one of `"object"`, `"array"`, `"str"`,
/// `"num"`, `"bool"`, `"null"`; the kernel's vocabulary, verbatim, and
/// `type_name`'s answer over a handle.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing` — unreachable for a module-built `Node`,
/// honest for a forged one. Never traps.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_type`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = json.root("{{\"a\": [1]}}")?
/// (json.type_of(r) else "?") == "object"
/// let a = json.member(r, "a")?
/// (json.type_of(a) else "?") == "array"
/// ```
pub fn type_of(n: Node) -> str ! {parse, missing} {
    json_type(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// The node rendered as text — `get`'s answer over a handle: a string
/// node comes back DECODED, a number keeps its source spelling, `true`,
/// `false` and `null` spell themselves, and a container comes back as
/// JSON text, **a valid document in its own right**. That last case is
/// how this half composes with `std.json`: `text_of` of a container is a
/// text its `parse` reads, exactly as its `stringify` writes a text
/// `root` reads (in prose because one program cannot import both — the
/// two modules share a leaf name, F-0058).
///
/// A nested container may be re-rendered compactly rather than sliced
/// from the source (the kernel's documented shape); it is the same
/// document either way.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing` — forged-`Node` tags, as on `type_of`.
/// Never traps.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = json.root("{{\"a\": [1, 2]}}")?
/// let a = json.member(r, "a")?
/// (json.text_of(a) else "?") == "[1,2]"
/// let t = json.text_of(a) else "?"
/// let sub = json.root(t)?
/// (json.len_of(sub) else -1) == 2
/// ```
pub fn text_of(n: Node) -> str ! {parse, missing} {
    json_get(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// How many elements an array node has, or members an object node has.
///
/// Rows: `kind` for a scalar — a number has no length, and answering 0
/// or 1 would be a guess about which question was asked (`len`'s rule,
/// over a handle); `parse`, `missing` for a forged `Node`. Never traps.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_len`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = json.root("[1, 2, 3]")?
/// (json.len_of(r) else -1) == 3
/// let empty = json.root("{{}}")?
/// (json.len_of(empty) else -1) == 0
/// let scalar = json.root("42")?
/// (json.len_of(scalar) else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn len_of(n: Node) -> int ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    json_len(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// The text of a string node, decoded.
///
/// Rows: `kind` when the node is not a string — no coercion, ever:
/// `str_of` of a number is a miss and not `"42"`, because a reader that
/// guesses across JSON's types loses the document's own information
/// (`str_at`'s rule, over a handle); `parse`, `missing` for a forged
/// `Node`. Never traps. `text_of` is the function that renders ANY node.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let n = json.node("{{\"s\": \"line\\nbreak\"}}", "s")?
/// (json.str_of(n) else "?") == "line\nbreak"
/// let wrong = json.node("{{\"s\": 1}}", "s")?
/// (json.str_of(wrong) else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn str_of(n: Node) -> str ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    str_at(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// The boolean a bool node holds.
///
/// Rows: `kind` for every non-boolean node — `0`, `""` and `null` are
/// all `kind`, no coercion (`bool_at`'s rule, over a handle); `parse`,
/// `missing` for a forged `Node`. Never traps.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let n = json.node("{{\"on\": true}}", "on")?
/// (json.bool_of(n) else false) == true
/// let off = json.node("{{\"on\": false}}", "on")?
/// (json.bool_of(off) else true) == false
/// let zero = json.node("{{\"on\": 0}}", "on")?
/// (json.bool_of(zero) else false) == false
/// ```
pub fn bool_of(n: Node) -> bool ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    bool_at(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// The integer a number node holds, exactly.
///
/// Rows: `kind` when the node is not a number OR is a number that is not
/// an integer (`1.5`, `1e3` and `1.0` are all `kind` — the document
/// wrote a non-integer and this function will not round one); `overflow`
/// (§10's mark) when the integer is outside `int` — a row and not a
/// trap, because the digits came out of a DOCUMENT; `parse`, `missing`
/// for a forged `Node`. Exactness is the point: an id beyond 2^53
/// survives `int_of`. All of it is `int_at`'s contract, over a handle.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let n = json.node("{{\"id\": 9007199254740993}}", "id")?
/// (json.int_of(n) else 0) == 9007199254740993
/// let fraction = json.node("[1.5]", "0")?
/// (json.int_of(fraction) else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn int_of(n: Node) -> int ! {parse, missing, kind, overflow} {
    int_at(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// Whether the node is JSON `null`.
///
/// A predicate over a node that EXISTS, so it answers for every node —
/// which is why it is not `kind`-rowed: "is this null" has a yes-or-no
/// answer whatever the kind (`is_null_at`'s rule, over a handle). Rows:
/// `parse`, `missing` for a forged `Node`. Never traps.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let n = json.node("{{\"a\": null}}", "a")?
/// (json.is_null(n) else false) == true
/// let zero = json.node("{{\"a\": 0}}", "a")?
/// (json.is_null(zero) else true) == false
/// ```
pub fn is_null(n: Node) -> bool ! {parse, missing} {
    is_null_at(n.doc, n.path)?
}

/// The integer a JSON number's source text denotes. Private.
///
/// `kind` when the text is not an integer spelling at all (a fraction, an
/// exponent, an empty digit run); `overflow` when it is one and does not
/// fit `int`.
///
/// The range check is arithmetic rather than a comparison against a
/// literal, because the one value that cannot be built positively is the
/// one this has to admit: `int_min` is `-(922337203685477580 * 10) - 8`,
/// so the negative branch never forms the positive magnitude at all.
fn integer_of(text: str) -> int ! {kind, overflow} {
    var body = text
    var negative = false
    if body.starts_with("-") {
        negative = true
        body = body[1..]
    }
    if !is_digit_run(body) {
        return kind
    }
    let width = body.len
    if width > 19 {
        return overflow
    }
    if width < 19 {
        let small = fmt.parse_int(body) else |_| { return kind }
        if negative {
            return -small
        }
        return small
    }
    let head_text = body[0..18]
    let tail_text = body[18..19]
    let head = fmt.parse_int(head_text) else |_| { return kind }
    let tail = fmt.parse_int(tail_text) else |_| { return kind }
    let head_limit: int = 922337203685477580
    var tail_limit: int = 7
    if negative {
        tail_limit = 8
    }
    if head > head_limit {
        return overflow
    }
    if head == head_limit {
        if tail > tail_limit {
            return overflow
        }
    }
    if negative {
        return -head * 10 - tail
    }
    head * 10 + tail
}

/// Whether `s` is one or more ASCII digits and nothing else. Private.
///
/// Written with the recoverable slice rather than the language's, even
/// though the kernel has already promised this text is ASCII: a
/// non-boundary here would be the kernel breaking its promise, and a
/// `bounds` trap is not how a library should report that (§9's rule about
/// whose offset it is, taken the conservative way at a tier boundary).
fn is_digit_run(s: str) -> bool {
    let n = s.len
    if n == 0 {
        return false
    }
    var i: int = 0
    while i < n {
        let c = s.get(i..i + 1) else ""
        if !is_digit(c) {
            return false
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    true
}

/// Whether `c` is one ASCII digit. Private.
fn is_digit(c: str) -> bool {
    if c == "" {
        return false
    }
    let alphabet = "0123456789"
    alphabet.contains(c)
}
